---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ProductSubNav from '../../../components/ProductSubNav.astro';

const SUBNAV = [
  { label: 'Overview', href: '/products/cordel-play' },
  { label: 'How It Plays', href: '/products/cordel-play/how-it-plays' },
  { label: 'Editions', href: '/products/cordel-play/editions' },
  { label: 'Components', href: '/products/cordel-play/components' },
  { label: 'Consent Architecture', href: '/products/cordel-play/consent-architecture' },
  { label: 'Development Status', href: '/products/cordel-play/development-status' },
  { label: 'Preorder', href: '/products/cordel-play/preorder' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Consent Architecture | Cordel Play | ByteLite LLC"
  description="How the Consent Cup works in Cordel Play: a deterministic participant-selection mechanism that resolves consent and compatibility without public rejection."
  keywords="Cordel Play consent, Consent Cup, Consent Sphere"
>
  <Header slot="header" />
  <section class="section-sm"><div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Cordel Play', href: '/products/cordel-play' }, { label: 'Consent Architecture' }]} /></div></section>
  <ProductSubNav items={SUBNAV} />

  <section class="ca-hero">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:820px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Consent Architecture</p>
      <h1>Consent is a game structure, not a side conversation.</h1>
      <p class="ca-p">
        Cordel Play treats consent and pacing as mechanics built into the board and the
        decks — not something the group has to negotiate outside the game.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container mech-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-bottom:.875rem;color:#e4e7f2;">When the Consent Cup Is Used</h3>
        <p>The Consent Cup is brought out only when a player lands on an activity that requires another participating player. It is not used on every turn and is not a general pause or session-ending device.</p>
        <img src="/cordel/cordel-play-consent-cup-final.webp" alt="The Cordel Play Consent Cup: a matte black cup with an engraved silver Cordel Play wordmark and a clear dome lid capturing a consent sphere" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;margin-top:1rem;object-fit:cover;border-radius:.5rem;" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-bottom:.875rem;color:#e4e7f2;">Voluntary Consent Entry</h3>
        <p>Any player who is comfortable participating in that specific activity with the player who landed on the space may place their Consent Sphere into the cup. Players who do not wish to participate simply do not enter a sphere. No explanation, public refusal, or justification is required.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.875rem;">This naturally respects each player's sexuality, gender preferences, attraction, familiarity, boundaries, and current comfort level.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-bottom:.875rem;color:#e4e7f2;">Consent and Selection Resolved Together</h3>
        <p>After all interested players have had the opportunity to enter their Consent Spheres, the cup selects one sphere to determine the participating player.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.875rem;">If nobody enters a sphere, that is a complete and valid resolution: the activity has no participating partner, and play continues according to the card or space instructions. Some "landmine" activities may intentionally create this possibility, making an empty cup an ordinary gameplay outcome rather than an awkward rejection.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:760px;">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Why the Consent Cup Exists</h2>
      <p class="ca-p">
        Cordel Play may include players of different genders, sexualities, relationship types,
        attraction preferences, familiarity levels, and personal boundaries. A player may be
        comfortable performing an activity with one person but not another, or may simply need
        more time before participating with someone.
      </p>
      <p class="ca-p" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        The Consent Cup resolves these situations through the normal mechanics of the game.
      </p>
      <p class="ca-p" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        When an activity requires another participant, interested players voluntarily place their
        Consent Spheres into the cup. The cup then selects from only those players who consented
        to that specific activity with that specific player.
      </p>
      <p class="ca-p" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        Anyone who does not enter a sphere remains outside the activity without being required to
        publicly decline or explain why. A player may also respond naturally with something such
        as, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m still getting to know you&mdash;maybe next time.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p class="ca-p" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        If no Consent Spheres are entered, the empty cup is itself the resolution. Nobody is
        pressured, nobody is singled out, and the game continues.
      </p>
      <p class="ca-p" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        The Consent Cup therefore performs two functions simultaneously:
      </p>
      <ol class="ca-p" style="margin-top:.75rem;padding-left:1.25rem;">
        <li>It confirms who is willing to participate.</li>
        <li style="margin-top:.5rem;">It neutrally determines which consenting player is selected.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="ca-p" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        That is the actual consent architecture: consent, compatibility, selection, and
        non-participation are all resolved inside the ordinary gameplay system rather than
        through forced negotiation or public rejection.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:760px;text-align:center;">
      <a href="/products/cordel-play/how-it-plays" class="btn btn-outline">See the Six Escalation Tiers &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>

<style>
.ca-hero { padding: 4.5rem 0 3.5rem; }
.ca-p { font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #a0a8c0; }
.mech-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1.25rem; }
@media (max-width: 767px) { .mech-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
